+# Experiment: vectorized (batched) generic join
+
+Branch `exp/vectorized-join`. Sketch: [#204 Batched GenericJoin](https://github.com/FiV0/triplox/issues/204).
+
+Bindings between join levels are stored in columns with structural sharing instead of one
+`Vec<Bytes>` per row. Same planner, same stage structure, same variable order, same row order.
+Off by default; `TRIPLOX_BATCHED_JOIN=1` turns it on.
+
+## Why: the profile
+
+`sample` on the release bench binary, `QUERY_FILTER=triangles RUNS=25`, 20s at 1ms.
+
+Two things to know before repeating this. Query work does **not** run on the thread called
+`triplox-incremental-query` — that one sits parked in `block_on`; the work is on a tokio worker
+(13964 samples). And `sample` prints `???` for every frame, so symbols have to come from
+`atos -o target/release/deps/datalog_bench-<hash> -l <load address>`.
+
+Self (leaf) time:
+
+| % | frame |
+|---|---|
+| 33.6 | libsystem_malloc |
+| 19.6 | libsystem_platform (memcpy/memmove/memcmp) |
+| 6.4 | `<Bytes as Ord>::cmp` |
+| 6.4 | `btree::search::search_tree` |
+| 6.1 | libsystem_kernel |
+| 1.9 | `<Vec<T> as Clone>::clone` |
+| 1.6 | memcmp stub |
+| 1.4 | `bytes::shared_clone` |
+| 1.3 | `bytes::release_shared` |
+| 1.2 | `RawVecInner::finish_grow` |
+
+Inclusive time, triplox frames:
+
+| % | frame |
+|---|---|
+| 99.1 | `GenericJoinEngine::execute` |
+| 91.8 | `TriplePattern::join` |
+| 16.0 | `TemporalFilterIterator::seek` |
+| 12.3 | `TemporalFilterIterator::next` |
+| 10.1 | `BindingBag::extend_rows` |
+| 3.5 | `drop_in_place<BindingBag>` |
+| 3.0 | `BindingBag::project` (the reorder after each propose) |
+| 2.4 | `TriplePattern::count` |
+
+About 55% of the triangles query is malloc, memcpy and `Bytes` comparison. Storage iteration
+through slatedb is ~28%. The allocation traffic is all bookkeeping the row layout forces:
+
+- `candidate_extensions` wraps **every candidate value in its own `Vec<Bytes>`**
+  (`vec![var_pair.slice(..)]`), then deep-clones the whole `Vec<Vec<Bytes>>` group **once per
+  input row** that shares the key.
+- `BindingBag::extend_rows` allocates a fresh `Vec<Bytes>` **per output row** and memcpies the
+  prefix into it.
+- the `reorder` after each propose allocates another `Vec<Bytes>` per row.
+- grouping input rows by their bound value goes through `BTreeMap<&Bytes, Vec<usize>>`, which is
+  the 6.4% `search_tree`, most of the 6.4% `Bytes::cmp`, and one `Vec<usize>` per distinct key.
+
+`count` is cold on triangles: single-proposer stages skip counting entirely.
+
+## What was built
+
+A columnar `Batch`: each level either owns a `Vec<Bytes>` column or points at its parent's column
+through a `Vec<u32>` row map. Extending a level allocates exactly two vectors — the parent-row map
+and the new value column — no matter how many rows come out. Selection composes row maps instead
+of copying, so a filter is one `Vec<u32>`.
+
+Grouping is a sort of row indices (`sorted_rows` + `for_each_group`) rather than a BTreeMap, so a
+group costs no allocation at all. A scan for a distinct key appends into one shared value pool and
+the rows of that group record the same `[start, end)` range into it, so a group is scanned once
+and never copied.
+
+`count_batch` estimates once per **distinct** key instead of once per row.
+
