+
+## A/B
+
+Interleaved off/on/off/on/off/on, three passes per arm, 5 timed runs per query per pass, on the
+shared harness (`VERTICES=2000 EDGE_PROB=0.01`, 39764 edges). `median` is the median of the three
+per-pass medians; `min` is the best of the three per-pass minima. The machine had four other agents
+building on it, so medians are noisy — the `min` column is the more trustworthy one, and anything
+under ~10 ms is noise either way.
+
+JSON: `results/vectorized-join-off.json`, `results/vectorized-join-on.json` (in the session
+scratchpad).
+
+| query | rows | off med | on med | Δ med | off min | on min | Δ min |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| triangles | 7821 | 748.45 | 437.58 | **-41.5%** | 581.32 | 386.07 | **-33.6%** |
+| two_hop_count | 1 | 294.74 | 179.43 | **-39.1%** | 232.11 | 162.06 | **-30.2%** |
+| three_hop_count | 1 | 11837.23 | 3294.70 | **-72.2%** | 7801.70 | 2310.78 | **-70.4%** |
+| out_degree | 2000 | 56.78 | 63.46 | +11.8% | 51.67 | 47.65 | -7.8% |
+| in_degree_top | 2000 | 60.74 | 57.70 | -5.0% | 51.74 | 48.35 | -6.6% |
+| neighbors_of_42 | 21 | 2.39 | 3.01 | +25.9% | 1.61 | 1.81 | +12.4% |
+| weight_filter | 198 | 6.32 | 5.60 | -11.4% | 4.70 | 4.20 | -10.6% |
+| weight_sum | 1 | 5.41 | 4.60 | -15.0% | 4.39 | 3.59 | -18.2% |
+| heavy_neighbors | 1908 | 80.04 | 76.72 | -4.1% | 75.44 | 67.35 | -10.7% |
+| label_lookup | 1 | 0.05 | 0.07 | +40.0% | 0.02 | 0.02 | 0.0% |
+
+Row counts are identical in both arms and match the hand-off baseline for all ten queries
+(triangles 7821, out_degree 2000, in_degree_top 2000, neighbors_of_42 21, weight_filter 198,
+heavy_neighbors 1908, the rest 1). `out_degree` disagrees between its two columns and
+`neighbors_of_42` / `label_lookup` are 2 ms and 20 µs queries; none of those three is a real
+signal.
+
+The gain tracks the number of join levels, which is what you would expect if the win is row
+bookkeeping: single-level queries are flat, two levels ~-30%, three levels ~-70%.
+
+## Correctness
+
+- `src/query/vectorized/tests.rs` runs 10 queries (triangles, two-hop, aggregates, predicates,
+  constant-value lookups, a pattern binding the same value on both endpoints) through both engines
+  and compares `variables` and `rows` **exactly, including row order**. The batched engine
+  reproduces the row engine's ordering rules: ascending-key group order, extensions grouped by
+  input row, multi-proposer shards grouped by proposer in first-occurrence order.
+- `cargo test -p triplox`: 610 lib + 32 integration tests pass. They also all pass with
+  `TRIPLOX_BATCHED_JOIN=1`, so every query in the existing suite that the batched engine claims
+  gives the same answer.
+- `cargo clippy -p triplox --all-targets`: clean.
+
+## What is missing
+
+1. **No bounded batches.** Issue #204 asks for levels that fill only to a budget. `Batch::chunks`
+   exists but the engine does not use it: a level is still fully materialized, just in columns
+   instead of rows. Peak memory drops (one `Vec<u32>` + one `Vec<Bytes>` per level instead of a
+   `Vec<Bytes>` per row) but it is still O(intermediate size). Pipelined chunking is safe to add
+   for single-proposer stages — chunks are contiguous ascending row ranges, so processing a chunk
+   through the remaining stages preserves the row order. It is *not* order-preserving across a
+   multi-proposer stage, because sharding by proposer is a global reordering.
+2. **No columnar sink.** `BatchedJoinEngine::execute` ends in `Batch::to_binding_bag`, which
+   allocates the very `Vec<Bytes>` per row the experiment set out to avoid. For the counting
+   queries that is the whole remaining cost: `three_hop_count` returns one row but materializes
+   the entire final level to get there. An aggregate/projection sink reading columns directly is
+   the largest remaining win and is a small change — `compile_find_plan` already knows the
+   column indices it needs.
+3. **No scan cache.** An unbound proposal re-scans the whole `AE`/`AV` range every time the
+   pattern proposes. `out_degree`-shaped queries pay this once so it does not show up here, but a
+   repeated full scan inside a multi-level plan would.
+4. **Only triple and predicate patterns.** `relation`, `function`, `not`, `or` do not implement
+   `BatchPattern`, so any query containing one falls back to the row engine wholesale — including
+   the parts that would have been batched. Rules and `or` are where a mixed engine gets awkward,
+   because `or` recursively executes sub-plans that return `BindingBag`s.
+5. **Multi-proposer stages materialize.** Sharding uses `Batch::concat`, which copies columns.
+   Preserving the row engine's shard ordering without a copy needs a batch that can hold several
+   parents; not attempted.
+6. The `TX_EID_OP_SUFFIX`/extractor logic and the six `validate` arms are duplicated between
+   `triple.rs`'s row and batch paths. Any full integration should delete the row path rather than
+   maintain both.
+
+## Verdict
+
+Worth landing. The profile said the row layout, not storage access, is what triangles spends its
+time on, and replacing the layout cut the multi-join queries by 30-70% with byte-identical
+results, no planner change, and no new storage format. The extension point is one defaulted trait
+method (`ExecPattern::as_batch`), so the two engines coexist without either knowing about the
+other.
+
+Integration cost, in rough order of value:
+
+- **Small (~a day):** columnar aggregate/projection sink. Removes the last per-row allocation and
+  should take another large bite out of the counting queries.
+- **Small:** `BatchPattern` for `relation` and `function`. Both are already row-shaped filters or
+  extensions over bound columns.
+- **Medium:** pipelined chunking with a row budget, restricted to prefixes of single-proposer
+  stages. This is the part issue #204 actually asked for and the part that changes the memory
+  story rather than the constant factor.
+- **Medium/large:** `not` and `or`, which need sub-plan execution to speak `Batch` rather than
+  `BindingBag`, and then deleting the row engine so the `validate` arms exist once.
+
+The risk is the duplicated `validate`/extractor logic surviving for long. Two implementations of
+six index-selection arms that must agree bug-for-bug is the kind of thing that quietly diverges;
+the equivalence test is the only thing holding them together today.
